WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.790
Look at a subway map.

2
00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:07.660
Stations, and the lines between them.

3
00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.540
Now a social network: people

4
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.980
and the friendships between them.

5
00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:13.900
And the web: pages

6
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.650
and the links between them.

7
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.990
Three very different things

8
00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:21.290
and one picture: dots and lines.

9
00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:23.000
Mathematicians call it a graph.

10
00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:26.219
A lot of the most useful questions in computing

11
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.540
are questions about graphs.

12
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:30.140
What is the fastest route?

13
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.280
Who is connected to whom?

14
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.190
Which pages matter?

15
00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.790
But before we can answer any of them

16
00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:39.530
a program has to store the graph somewhere.

17
00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:40.500
How?

18
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:42.450
This is the first video

19
00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:43.960
of the graph chapter.

20
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:45.709
We'll see the words we use

21
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:47.160
to talk about graphs

22
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.700
three ways to store one

23
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.470
and how to choose between them.

24
00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:55.060
A graph has vertices, the dots, and edges

25
00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.450
the lines between two vertices.

26
00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.730
An edge can be undirected

27
00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:02.210
like a friendship that goes both ways.

28
00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:03.430
Or directed

29
00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.170
like a link from one page to another

30
00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:08.000
which only goes one way.

31
00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:12.180
An edge can also carry a number, a weight

32
00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:15.830
a distance, a price, a travel time.

33
00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:18.151
The degree of a vertex is

34
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.980
how many edges touch it.

35
00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:23.452
A path is a walk along edges.

36
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.433
A cycle is a path that comes

37
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:26.827
back to where it started.

38
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.807
A graph is connected when there is a

39
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.130
path between every two vertices.

40
00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.286
Here is the graph we will use

41
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:34.640
in this whole chapter.

42
00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.880
Seven vertices, A to G

43
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.320
and eleven undirected edges

44
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.510
each with a weight.

45
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.050
Count the degrees.

46
00:01:42.050 --> 00:01:48.590
A has two, B four, C two, D four, E five

47
00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.740
F three, G two.

48
00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.440
Add them up: twenty-two.

49
00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.580
That is exactly twice the number of edges

50
00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.330
eleven.

51
00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:59.030
Not a coincidence.

52
00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.049
Every edge has two ends

53
00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.168
so every edge adds one to

54
00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:04.513
two different degrees.

55
00:02:04.570 --> 00:02:06.975
The sum of the degrees is always

56
00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.760
twice the number of edges.

57
00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.450
The simplest way to store a graph

58
00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.110
a list of its edges.

59
00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.140
Each entry says: from, to, and the weight.

60
00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.200
Eleven edges, eleven entries.

61
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:21.530
Very compact.

62
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.730
But ask it a question.

63
00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:26.590
Is there an edge between A and G?

64
00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.050
You have to read the whole list to be sure.

65
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.350
And who are the neighbours of E?

66
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.520
Again, the whole list.

67
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.990
A better idea: for every vertex

68
00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:38.670
keep a list of its neighbours.

69
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:40.900
This is the adjacency list.

70
00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.020
A has B and D.

71
00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.903
B has A, C, D and E.

72
00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:47.809
Notice that an undirected

73
00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.570
edge appears twice: once in each end's list.

74
00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:54.310
The neighbours of a vertex are right there

75
00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:55.632
in its own list.

76
00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.257
Looking at them costs as many steps

77
00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:01.000
as the vertex has neighbours, no more.

78
00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.920
Is there an edge from A to G?

79
00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.220
Look through A's short list.

80
00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.150
Two entries, and G is not there.

81
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.430
The lists hold twenty-two entries in all.

82
00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:14.260
Twice the number of edges.

83
00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.861
A third way: a table with a row and a column

84
00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:19.082
for every vertex.

85
00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:21.855
The cell in row u and column v holds

86
00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:23.950
the weight of the edge from u to v

87
00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:25.850
or stays empty if there is none.

88
00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:28.640
This is the adjacency matrix.

89
00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:31.480
Is there an edge from A to G?

90
00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.220
Go straight to that cell.

91
00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.110
One step, however big the graph is.

92
00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:38.830
Because the edge is undirected

93
00:03:38.830 --> 00:03:40.232
the table is symmetric

94
00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.627
the cell for B and A matches

95
00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:44.660
the cell for A and B.

96
00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:48.340
The price: seven rows of seven cells

97
00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:49.540
forty-nine cells

98
00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.880
even though there are only eleven edges.

99
00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.860
And to list the neighbours of one vertex

100
00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:56.380
you must scan its whole row

101
00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:57.850
all seven cells.

102
00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:00.360
Let's put the three side by side

103
00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.460
for our small graph.

104
00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:05.940
Entries stored: eleven, twenty-two

105
00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.190
forty-nine.

106
00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:09.790
Is there an edge from u to v?

107
00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:12.070
The matrix: one step.

108
00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:15.710
The list: as many steps as u has neighbours.

109
00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:18.460
The edge list: every edge.

110
00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:20.540
Who are the neighbours of v?

111
00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:23.040
The list: exactly its neighbours.

112
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.460
The matrix: the whole row.

113
00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.970
The edge list: every edge.

114
00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:30.380
Pause and predict.

115
00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.900
A social network has a thousand people

116
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.380
and each follows about six others.

117
00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.070
About three thousand friendships.

118
00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.040
How many cells does the

119
00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.908
adjacency matrix need?

120
00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:43.067
And how many entries does

121
00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:44.980
the adjacency list need?

122
00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:47.720
The matrix needs a million cells

123
00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.460
nearly all of them empty.

124
00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.770
The list needs only six thousand entries.

125
00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:56.250
So the matrix is not the safe choice.

126
00:04:56.250 --> 00:04:57.610
For a graph like this

127
00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.520
it wastes almost everything.

128
00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.769
Here are real counts, for that sparse graph.

129
00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:05.263
The list stores six thousand entries

130
00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:08.450
and answers has-edge in six steps.

131
00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:11.350
The matrix stores a million cells

132
00:05:11.350 --> 00:05:13.650
it answers has-edge in one step

133
00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.380
but listing neighbours costs a thousand.

134
00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:18.634
Now a dense graph

135
00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.019
a thousand vertices where half of all

136
00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:22.780
possible pairs are connected

137
00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.610
about two hundred fifty thousand edges.

138
00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:27.808
The list now holds nearly five

139
00:05:27.870 --> 00:05:29.190
hundred thousand entries

140
00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:30.950
and its lists are long.

141
00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:34.110
The matrix still holds a million cells

142
00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:35.650
but now half of them are full

143
00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:37.640
so it is not wasteful any more.

144
00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:40.360
The rule: for a sparse graph

145
00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:41.720
use adjacency lists.

146
00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:43.160
For a dense graph

147
00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:46.160
or when you must test edges constantly

148
00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.720
the matrix can win.

149
00:05:47.720 --> 00:05:49.830
Most real graphs are sparse.

150
00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:51.620
To sum up.

151
00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:54.220
A graph is vertices and edges

152
00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:57.340
directed or not, with or without weights.

153
00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:00.170
The degrees add up to twice the edges.

154
00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:04.490
Edge list, adjacency list, adjacency matrix

155
00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:06.001
they differ in space

156
00:06:06.070 --> 00:06:09.179
and in how fast they answer is-there-an-edge

157
00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:11.200
and who-are-the-neighbours.

158
00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.980
We will use adjacency lists.

159
00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:16.960
Next: how to walk through a graph

160
00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:19.910
with breadth-first and depth-first search.

161
00:06:20.537 --> 00:06:23.147
Let's build a graph class in Python

162
00:06:23.147 --> 00:06:24.617
and count the steps.

163
00:06:25.197 --> 00:06:26.947
The graph is a dictionary.

164
00:06:26.947 --> 00:06:28.727
Each key is a vertex

165
00:06:28.727 --> 00:06:31.047
and each value is a list of pairs

166
00:06:31.047 --> 00:06:31.827
a neighbour

167
00:06:31.827 --> 00:06:34.037
and the weight of the edge to it.

168
00:06:34.387 --> 00:06:37.117
Adding an edge appends to the list of u.

169
00:06:37.117 --> 00:06:39.297
And if the graph is not directed

170
00:06:39.297 --> 00:06:41.507
it also appends to the list of v.

171
00:06:41.857 --> 00:06:43.383
Forget that second line

172
00:06:43.447 --> 00:06:45.776
and your undirected graph quietly

173
00:06:45.847 --> 00:06:47.657
becomes a directed one.

174
00:06:48.237 --> 00:06:51.159
Neighbours: we return the list, sorted

175
00:06:51.227 --> 00:06:53.216
so that every traversal in this chapter

176
00:06:53.267 --> 00:06:54.957
visits in the same order.

177
00:06:55.307 --> 00:06:57.907
has-edge scans the list of u.

178
00:06:57.997 --> 00:07:00.610
The counter steps goes up once

179
00:07:00.697 --> 00:07:02.137
per entry looked at.

180
00:07:02.137 --> 00:07:04.097
That is the cost we talked about

181
00:07:04.097 --> 00:07:06.867
as many steps as u has neighbours.

182
00:07:07.217 --> 00:07:09.977
Degree is just the length of the list.

183
00:07:10.557 --> 00:07:13.907
The matrix version keeps a list of lists.

184
00:07:13.907 --> 00:07:16.037
has-edge is one lookup.

185
00:07:16.387 --> 00:07:18.410
Neighbours scan the whole row

186
00:07:18.477 --> 00:07:19.919
so the counter goes up by

187
00:07:19.977 --> 00:07:21.667
the number of vertices.

188
00:07:22.247 --> 00:07:23.669
Here are real counts.

189
00:07:23.737 --> 00:07:26.070
On the sparse graph with a thousand

190
00:07:26.137 --> 00:07:28.117
vertices and three thousand edges

191
00:07:28.117 --> 00:07:30.857
the list holds six thousand entries

192
00:07:30.857 --> 00:07:33.117
the matrix a million

193
00:07:33.117 --> 00:07:35.487
the edge list three thousand.

194
00:07:35.837 --> 00:07:39.307
Has-edge: six steps for the list

195
00:07:39.307 --> 00:07:41.467
one for the matrix

196
00:07:41.467 --> 00:07:43.907
three thousand for the edge list.

197
00:07:43.907 --> 00:07:49.247
Neighbours of one vertex: six, a thousand

198
00:07:49.247 --> 00:07:50.237
three thousand.

199
00:07:50.587 --> 00:07:52.992
On the dense graph, the list is long

200
00:07:53.057 --> 00:07:54.828
so it needs four hundred ninety-nine

201
00:07:54.877 --> 00:07:56.437
steps for has-edge.

202
00:07:56.437 --> 00:07:58.547
The matrix is still one.

203
00:07:59.127 --> 00:08:01.214
Python has no graph class

204
00:08:01.297 --> 00:08:02.937
in its standard library.

205
00:08:02.937 --> 00:08:04.657
A dictionary of lists

206
00:08:04.657 --> 00:08:06.697
or a defaultdict of lists

207
00:08:06.697 --> 00:08:07.916
is what people use.

208
00:08:07.977 --> 00:08:09.794
Third-party libraries such as

209
00:08:09.857 --> 00:08:12.167
networkx wrap the same idea.

210
00:08:12.747 --> 00:08:15.877
So in Python: a dictionary of lists

211
00:08:15.877 --> 00:08:18.417
store undirected edges twice

212
00:08:18.417 --> 00:08:21.227
the counter confirms the costs.

213
00:08:21.577 --> 00:08:26.017
Next: walking the graph with BFS and DFS.

